07/16/26|07/16/26||CDT|0|F|Y|Chicago|IL|41.8410|-87.6745|PM2.5||Hazardous|Yes|(Discussion Issued 7/16/26 10:25pm) The Illinois Environmental Protection Agency issued a Red Forecast Air Pollution Action Day for Friday July 17th for the AirNow AQI forecast sectors of Rockford and all 6 Chicago area forecast sectors.   This Air Pollution Action Day is declared due to forecasts for daily 24-hour average particulates anticipated to reach or exceed the Unhealthy-Red AQI category on Friday July 17th.   While the AQI forecasts are based on 24-hour averages of particulates, hourly NowCast AQI is likely to show categories higher than Unhealthy-Red in the morning hours of the 17th to help identify areas and timeframes of greater concern.  As long as smoke guidance models verify, particulate values should begin fall considerably throughout the mid-morning hours and into the early afternoon.  Likewise, NowCast AQI should also fall more towards the afternoon leading to lower 24-hour daily concentrations.  If this guidance does not verify, AQI forecasts will once again be updated accordingly. Looking past Friday, there are early indications of another wave of surface smoke approaching Illinois Saturday.  Although intensity, duration, and timing are unclear, early indications are for values that are less intense compared to current effects on air quality. AQI Category Guidance Unhealthy - Sensitive groups: Avoid long or intense outdoor activities. Consider rescheduling or moving activities indoors.  Everyone else: Reduce long or intense activities. Take more breaks during outdoor activities. Very Unhealthy - Sensitive groups: Avoid all physical activity outdoors. Reschedule to a time when air quality is better or move activities indoors. Everyone else: Avoid long or intense activities. Consider rescheduling or moving activities indoors.  Note: If you don't have an air conditioner, staying inside with the windows closed may be dangerous in extremely hot weather. If you are hot, go someplace with air conditioning or check with your local government to find out if cooling centers are available in your community. Hazardous - Everyone: Avoid all physical activity outdoors.  Sensitive groups: Remain indoors and keep activity levels low. Follow tips for keeping particle levels low indoors. Further south in the rest of Illinois, surface smoke is less likely but it is possible smoke moves further south than expected for shorter durations of time.  Those outside of Red and higher category forecast areas are also encouraged to periodically check current NowCast readings that would indicate surface smoke or sign up for EnviroFlash alerts on AirNow.gov   Note, NowCast AQI readings may show categories higher than the daily forecast to help identify periods of higher 1-hour PM2.5/smoke.  For a description of the differences between daily AQI forecasts and NowCast AQI, visit https://www.airnow.gov/aqi/aqi-basics/using-air-quality-index/ .|Illinois EPA
