07/16/26|07/18/26||EDT|2|F|Y|Philadelphia|PA|39.9500|-75.1510|PM2.5|130|Unhealthy for Sensitive Groups|No|The Department is issuing a STATEWIDE CODE PURPLE Air Quality Alert (AQA) for Friday for fine particulate matter (PM2.5). Wildfire smoke continues to impact Pennsylvania from Ontario, Canada and Minnesota, significantly deteriorating the air quality across Commonwealth.  The flow continues from the northwest this Thursday afternoon, but is expected to eventually move out of the area on Saturday.  Substantial concentrations of PM2.5 will be seen, including at least some locations seeing hourly values in the hazardous range.  For the northeastern counties, there is a potential that PM2.5 levels are lower than Code Purple, but we are being proactively cautious as this relates to public health.  Please note that the PM2.5 health-based standard is based on a 24 hour average, where individual hours will have concentrations higher (and lower) than the forecast. We forecast PM2.5 using this 24 hour framework to help the public understand when overall daily air quality may reach unhealthy levels to sensitive groups (USG – Code Orange), unhealthy for all (Code Red), very unhealthy (Code Purple) and hazardous (Code Maroon) and to guide actions that reduce exposure. DEP will monitor the ongoing smoke conditions.  Current Conditions at 2 p.m. EDT this Thursday afternoon: Smoke emanating from wildfires across southern Canada and the northern Great Lakes are sending PM2.5 concentrations into the code RED range (Unhealthy). Ozone levels are also rising well close to the code ORANGE range as temperature rise into the lower 90s. Heavy smoke will continue to impact air quality across the area Friday, but may abate some over the weekend. Here are the forecast details:  ***  Friday’s forecast: Smoke levels will continue to be intense Friday, with concentrations rising to code PURPLE (Very Unhealthy) category. Hazy sunshine will send high temperatures to around 90 degrees. High moderate ozone is again likely during the afternoon. People sensitive to air pollution and smoke should limit or postpone outdoor activity. Visit www.airnow.gov for the latest air quality information in your location. *** Extended forecast: The smoke may abate some Saturday, however, PM2.5 readings may remain in the code ORANGE or Unhealthy for Sensitive Groups range for a good part of the day prior to showers and thunderstorms arriving as low pressure influences the local weather. Morning clouds giving way to some sunshine Sunday with moderate PM2.5 forecast. We will continue to monitor the progress of any wildfire smoke over the next several days with forecast updates as needed.---McAuliffe/Oshinski|Pennsylvania Dept. of Environmental Protection
